    action: '<a href="/go/any-do" rel="nofollow sponsored">Get Any.do</a>',
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What is the best productivity app for remote workers?",
    answer:
      '<p><strong>Notion</strong> is our top overall pick for remote workers. It combines notes, tasks, databases, and wikis in one flexible workspace. For pure task management, <strong>Todoist</strong> is unbeatable—simple, fast, and available everywhere. <strong>Obsidian</strong> is best for knowledge workers who take extensive notes.</p><p>The "best" app depends on your workflow. If you need team collaboration, Notion excels. For personal task management, Todoist or Things 3 shine. For building a personal knowledge base, Obsidian is unmatched.</p>',
  },
  {
    question: "Is Notion better than Todoist?",
    answer:
      "<p>They serve different purposes and excel at different things. <strong>Notion</strong> is an all-in-one workspace—notes, databases, docs, and tasks combined. It's powerful but has a learning curve. <strong>Todoist</strong> is laser-focused on task management—simpler, faster, and more reliable for pure to-do lists.</p><p>Choose Notion if you want one app for everything and enjoy customization. Choose Todoist if you want the best pure task manager that stays out of your way. Many power users use both together.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Are free productivity apps good enough?",
    answer:
      "<p>Yes, for many users. <strong>Notion's free tier</strong> is incredibly generous—unlimited pages and blocks for personal use. <strong>Todoist Free</strong> handles 5 projects and basic features well. <strong>Obsidian</strong> is completely free for local use.</p><p>Premium tiers add team collaboration, advanced features, and priority support. Solo remote workers can often stick with free tiers indefinitely; teams typically need paid plans for sharing and permissions.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I use a task manager or a note-taking app?",
    answer:
      "<p>Ideally, both—they serve different needs. <strong>Task managers</strong> (Todoist, Things 3, TickTick) excel at capturing, organizing, and tracking actionable items with due dates and priorities. <strong>Note apps</strong> (Obsidian, Notion, Evernote) store reference material, meeting notes, ideas, and documentation.</p><p>Some apps blur the line: Notion and TickTick handle both reasonably well. But dedicated tools often outperform all-in-one solutions in their specific domain.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the GTD method and which apps support it?",
    answer:
      "<p><strong>GTD (Getting Things Done)</strong> is David Allen's productivity methodology built around capturing everything, clarifying actions, organizing by context, and regular reviews. Core concepts include next actions, projects, contexts (@work, @home), and weekly reviews.</p><p><strong>Best GTD apps:</strong> Things 3 (built around GTD), OmniFocus (power-user GTD), Todoist (excellent GTD support with labels and filters), TickTick (good GTD features). Notion can be configured for GTD but requires setup.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Evernote still worth using in 2025?",
    answer:
      "<p>Evernote has had a difficult few years—pricing increases, feature removals, and strong competition. However, it still excels at <strong>web clipping</strong>—capturing articles, PDFs, and web content is unmatched. The search (including text in images) remains excellent.</p><p>For new users, Notion or Obsidian offer better value. For existing Evernote users with large databases, migration is painful. If web clipping is your primary use case, Evernote is still the best choice.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the best productivity app for Apple users?",
    answer:
      "<p><strong>Things 3</strong> is the gold standard for Apple-exclusive users. It's beautifully designed, deeply integrated with iOS/macOS, and incredibly fast. The one-time purchase ($50 Mac + $10 iPhone) avoids subscription fatigue.</p><p>If you need cross-platform access (Android, Windows, Web), choose Todoist or TickTick instead. Things 3 syncs only between Apple devices via iCloud.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I avoid productivity app overload?",
    answer:
      "<p>The best system is the one you'll actually use. Start with <strong>one app</strong> and commit to it for at least 30 days before adding tools. Resist the urge to try every new app—constant switching destroys productivity.</p><p>A solid minimal stack: one task manager (Todoist), one note app (Obsidian or Notion), one calendar. That's it. Add tools only when you have a specific problem they solve. Most people need fewer apps, not more.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Project Management Software for Remote Teams",
    excerpt:
      "Compare Notion, ClickUp, Asana, and more to find the perfect PM tool for your distributed team.",
    href: "/tools/best-project-management-software",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Modern workspace with laptop displaying project management dashboard",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Time Tracking Software for Freelancers",
    excerpt:
      "Track billable hours accurately with our top picks for freelancer-friendly time tracking tools.",
    href: "/tools/best-time-tracking-software",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1508962914676-134849a727f0-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Clock and timer concept for time tracking",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Team Communication Tools",
    excerpt:
      "Keep your distributed team connected with these top-rated communication and collaboration platforms.",
    href: "/blog/best-team-communication-tools",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1600880292203-757bb62b4baf-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Remote team on video call",
